From: Ziy engine fork <ziy@ziy.app>
Date: 2026-10-15
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] RFP: restore the -CSSPrefersColorScheme carve-out in RFP mode

Ziy locks privacy.resistFingerprinting=true and ships
privacy.fingerprintingProtection.overrides="+AllTargets,-CSSPrefersColorScheme"
(shell/mozilla.cfg, ziy.js). The intent is "every protection except the
colour-scheme spoof, which breaks dark-mode stylesheets".

The carve-out does not take effect, and it cannot: in RFP mode
nsRFPService::IsRFPEnabledFor() returns true for every target before it ever
consults the override set. The overrides pref is only read in FPP/Baseline
mode, so the pref layer's carve-out is dead code the moment
resistFingerprinting is on. This is exactly the "behaviour the RFP pref layer
cannot express" that replica/deep-fork.md §6.6 names as the preferred first
C++ patch target.

Behaviour is restored by reading the engine's own overrides pref in
HandleExceptionalRFPTargets(), which IsRFPEnabledFor() consults FIRST, and
returning false for CSSPrefersColorScheme when the pref contains the
-CSSPrefersColorScheme term. Nothing else changes: every other target keeps
its RFP-mode spoof, and the carve-out is inert unless the pref carries it.

Gated behind MOZ_ZIY_FINGERPRINTING, set by the
--enable-ziy-fingerprinting configure option (see the second patch), so a
build with the gate off is stock Gecko.

Sources for every claim in this commit message:
  toolkit/components/resistfingerprinting/nsRFPService.cpp:335 IsRFPEnabledFor
  toolkit/components/resistfingerprinting/nsRFPService.cpp:296
    HandleExceptionalRFPTargets
  toolkit/components/resistfingerprinting/nsRFPService.cpp:323
    IsTargetActiveForMode (the RFP-mode `return true`)
  dom/base/Document.cpp:21177 Document::PreferredColorScheme (the consumer)
  RFPTargets.inc:136 the documented "+AllTargets,-CSSPrefersColorScheme" form
---
--- a/toolkit/components/resistfingerprinting/nsRFPService.cpp
+++ b/toolkit/components/resistfingerprinting/nsRFPService.cpp
@@ -299,6 +299,41 @@
   MOZ_ASSERT(GetFingerprintingProtectionType(aIsPrivateMode) !=
              FingerprintingProtectionType::None);
 
+#ifdef MOZ_ZIY_FINGERPRINTING
+  // Ziy delta 1: the per-target carve-out that prefs cannot reach.
+  //
+  // WHY A C++ CHANGE IS REQUIRED. Ziy locks these prefs (shell/mozilla.cfg
+  // and ziy.js): privacy.resistFingerprinting=true and
+  // privacy.fingerprintingProtection.overrides="+AllTargets,-CSSPrefersColorScheme".
+  // The intent of that string is "run every protection except the colour-scheme
+  // spoof, which breaks dark-mode stylesheets".
+  //
+  // It does not work, and the reason is the RFP-mode short-circuit further down
+  // in this same function: when privacy.resistFingerprinting is on, the mode is
+  // FingerprintingProtectionType::RFP and IsRFPEnabledFor() returns true for
+  // EVERY target BEFORE it ever looks at the override set. The overrides pref is
+  // only consulted in FPP/Baseline mode, so the pref layer's carve-out is dead
+  // code the moment resistFingerprinting is on. There is no pref, no JSON array,
+  // and no granular override that can turn off exactly one target while RFP mode
+  // is on — that is the "behaviour the RFP pref layer cannot express" that
+  // deep-fork.md §6.6 names as the preferred first C++ patch.
+  //
+  // This restores the carve-out's intended effect in RFP mode. It is deliberately
+  // narrow: it only removes the colour-scheme spoof, only when the engine's own
+  // overrides pref carries the -CSSPrefersColorScheme carve-out, and only for
+  // the RFP mode that currently ignores overrides entirely.
+  if (aMode == FingerprintingProtectionType::RFP &&
+      aTarget == RFPTarget::CSSPrefersColorScheme) {
+    nsAutoString overrides;
+    if (NS_SUCCEEDED(Preferences::GetString(
+            RESIST_FINGERPRINTINGPROTECTION_OVERRIDE_PREF, overrides))) {
+      if (overrides.Find(u"-CSSPrefersColorScheme") != kNotFound) {
+        return Some(false);
+      }
+    }
+  }
+#endif  // MOZ_ZIY_FINGERPRINTING
+
   // IsAlwaysEnabledForPrecompute is used to enable fingerprinting protections.
   // It isn't included in the RFPTargetSet.
   if (aTarget == RFPTarget::IsAlwaysEnabledForPrecompute) {
